---
import { withBase } from '../lib/base';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
// dedent() is used to so that <pre> renders with correct indentation
import { dedent } from '../lib/dedent';

const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/mq-cpc/mq-cpc.github.io';
---
<Base title="Contribute — MQ CP Club" description="How to change the MQ CP Club website: the content lives as Markdown in the repo, and every change is a pull request.">
  <Header />
  <main class="mq-page">
    <div class="mono mq-eyebrow">// CONTRIBUTE</div>
    <h1 class="ps-h1 mq-h1-gap">How to contribute</h1>
    <p class="text-ink max-w-measure mt-3">
      This site is built with <b>Astro</b>, a static-site generator: the content — topics, problems, events, resources — 
      lives as Markdown and JSON files, and Astro
      compiles it all to plain HTML. 
      Every change, from fixing a typo to adding a problem, 
      is a <a class="text-accent-text no-underline" href="https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/reference/pull-requests">pull request</a> that 
      an admin reviews and merges. 
      The references on how to change the website are shown below.
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink max-w-measure mt-3">
      The repo is public, so you don't need to be added to anything to propose a change — GitHub
      makes you a copy to work in. Ask an admin for access to the club org only if you want to
      review and merge other people's changes.
    </p>

    <a class="ps-btn ps-btn--primary mt-4" href={repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Open the club repo →
    </a>

    {/* Anchor target: the Events page links here from both its "New to pull
        requests?" links, since this is the how-a-PR-works content. */}
    <h3 id="pull-request" class="ps-h3 mq-heading mq-heading--mt6">Step by step</h3>
    <ol class="text-ink max-w-measure leading-loose pl-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
      <li>Use 
       the <b>where things live</b> card below to work out which file you need to modify or where to add a new file.
      </li>
      <li>Click the pencil (<b>Edit this file</b>) and make your change.</li>
      <li>Scroll to <b>Commit changes…</b>, keep <b>Create a new branch and start a pull request</b> selected, then click <b>Propose changes</b>.</li>
      <li>On the next screen, click <b>Create pull request</b>. Done — a committee member reviews and merges it, and the site rebuilds itself.</li>
    </ol>


    <div class="mt-7">
      <div class="mq-panel">
        <div class="mq-panel__title">Where things live</div>
        <dl class="mq-fields">
          <div><dt class="mono">src/content/topics/</dt><dd>roadmap topics: blurb, concept videos, and the sections problems are grouped under</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="mono">src/content/problems/</dt><dd>one file per problem; notes go in the body</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="mono">src/content/events/</dt><dd>practice sessions and contests</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="mono">src/content/reference/</dt><dd>team reference notebook snippets</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="mono">src/content/resources/</dt><dd>curated link groups</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="mono">src/data/team-board.json</dt><dd>the looking-for-a-team board</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="mono">src/data/site.json</dt><dd>club contact and meeting details</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="add-a-problem" class="ps-h2 mq-heading mq-heading--mt8">Add a problem</h2>
    {/* The steps are a sequence you read top to bottom; the field glossary is
        reference you consult while writing the file, so it earns the companion
        column. */}
    <div class="mq-split">
      <div>
        <p class="text-ink max-w-measure">
          To add a problem, create a Markdown file in{' '}
          <span class="mono">src/content/problems/</span>, named
          after the problem's id at the judge — <span class="mono">freefood.md</span> for{' '}
          <span class="mono">open.kattis.com/problems/freefood</span>. Everything the topic page
          shows comes from its front matter:
        </p>
        <pre class="mono mt-3 p-4 bg-paper-sunk border border-line rounded-md text-label overflow-x-auto">{dedent(`
          ---
          topic: java-basics
          title: "Free Food"
          difficulty: easy
          judge: Kattis
          url: https://open.kattis.com/problems/freefood
          order: 11
          tags: ["arrays"]
          ---
          Anything you write below the front matter shows up as
          notes when the problem is expanded. Leave it empty
          if you have none.
        `)}</pre>

        <h3 class="ps-h3 mq-heading mq-heading--mt6">Which section it lands under</h3>
        <p class="text-ink max-w-measure">
          A topic page groups its problems under headings the topic itself declares — Getting
          Started has <b>Video Tutorials</b> and <b>Problems</b>.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-ink max-w-measure leading-loose pl-5 mt-3">
          <li>Give it a <span class="mono">videos:</span> walkthrough and it joins the <b>first</b> section — the video one.</li>
          <li>Give it none and it joins the <b>last</b> section, where the problems you solve unaided live.</li>
          <li>Add <span class="mono">section: arrays</span> to put it somewhere specific, whether or not it has a video.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-ink max-w-measure mt-3">
          The ids you can use are listed in the topic's own file, under{' '}
          <span class="mono">sections:</span> — open it and read them off:
        </p>
        <pre class="mono mt-3 p-4 bg-paper-sunk border border-line rounded-md text-label overflow-x-auto">{dedent(`
          # src/content/topics/java-basics.md
          sections:
            - id: tutorials
              title: Video Tutorials
            - id: arrays
              title: Arrays / ArrayList
            - id: extra
              title: Additional Problems
        `)}</pre>
        <p class="text-ink max-w-measure mt-3">
          Naming an id the topic doesn't declare fails the build with an error saying so, rather
          than quietly dropping your problem off the page. A section with no problems in it isn't
          rendered at all, so it's fine for a topic to declare one it doesn't use yet.
        </p>

        <h3 class="ps-h3 mq-heading mq-heading--mt6">Adding a new section</h3>
        <p class="text-ink max-w-measure">
          Add an entry to <span class="mono">sections:</span> in the topic file: an{' '}
          <span class="mono">id</span> (letters and dashes, used as the link anchor), a{' '}
          <span class="mono">title</span> shown as the heading, and an optional{' '}
          <span class="mono">intro</span> paragraph. The intro is Markdown, so links and{' '}
          <span class="mono">code</span> work in it. Sections render in the order you list them, and
          a topic that declares none falls back to <b>Video Tutorials</b> then{' '}
          <b>Additional Problems</b>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="mq-aside">
        <div class="mq-panel">
          <div class="mq-panel__title">What the fields mean</div>
          <dl class="mq-fields">
            <div><dt class="mono">topic</dt><dd>the topic file's name, e.g. java-basics</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">title</dt><dd>the problem's title at the judge, copied exactly</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">difficulty</dt><dd>easy, medium or hard</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">judge</dt><dd>Kattis, CodingBat, …</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">url</dt><dd>link to the problem</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">order</dt><dd>sorts the problem within its section</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">tags</dt><dd>optional; become the filter chips on the topic page</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">section</dt><dd>optional; the section id, overriding the video rule</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">videos</dt><dd>optional; walkthroughs, shown when the problem is expanded</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <h2 class="ps-h2 mq-heading mq-heading--mt8">Running it locally</h2>
    <p class="text-ink max-w-measure">
      Only worth it if you're changing the code rather than the content. Clone the repo,{' '}
      <span class="mono">npm install</span>, then <span class="mono">npm run dev</span>.{' '}
      <span class="mono">README.md</span> and <span class="mono">CLAUDE.md</span> in the repo are
      the accurate map of how the site is laid out.
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink max-w-measure mt-3">
      Looking for teammates or posting a session? Both live on the{' '}
      <a class="mono text-accent-text no-underline" href={withBase("/events")}>Events</a> page.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
